# fixedbugs, defect 066, panels 166 to 171, closed 2026-09-21.
#
# SYMPTOM: `check` exit 0, no diagnostic, and C reads a dead frame at exit 0.
# `--sanitize` says `stack-use-after-scope` when the retainer is a `static
# inline` in a header, and says NOTHING against the real `libsqlite3.dylib`,
# where the program prints the correct value and the instrument masks the
# defect. `s.cstr()` had the identical hole, `heap-use-after-free`, and it was
# the half the corpus used: 59 occurrences against `.ptr()`'s 33.
#
# CAUSE: nothing in the language related C's retention to the moment the bytes
# die. A lend lives for its call and no longer, and no declaration could say
# that a parameter KEEPS what it is handed, so a lend that reached one was
# admitted like any other and the corruption happened after the call, where no
# rule about the call could see it.
#
# FIX: the default flipped (the author's ruling of 2026-09-20; panel 171). A C
# parameter is assumed to keep unless declared `lent`, and a lend reaches only
# one so declared, so both halves of the reproducer are refused at `check`,
# each with the word to write and the copy to make instead. `later()` is quiet:
# what C hands back is not a lend, and that shape is written down as what the
# flip leaves standing.

extern "keeper.h"
    record Sl2
        name: u8[8]
        id: i64
    function mk() -> Sl2
    function keep(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64)
    function keep_label(s: cstr)
    function later() -> i64

function lend_and_return()
    s: Sl2 @ mk()
    keep(p: s.name.ptr(), n: 8)  #~ lend_kept

function lend_a_string()
    label = "H" + "payload"
    keep_label(s: label.cstr())  #~ lend_kept

function main()
    lend_and_return()
    lend_a_string()
    print(later())
